<section class="seo-section" aria-label="About GridPaw">
  <h1>GridPaw: Free <span class="accent">Shikaku</span> Puzzle Game Online</h1>
  <p>GridPaw is a <strong>free Shikaku puzzle game</strong> with adorable cat visuals. Draw rectangles on a numbered grid so each rectangle covers exactly the number of cells shown. It's the classic <strong>Shikaku</strong> (rectangle partition) puzzle by Nikoli — the same company behind Sudoku — reimagined with cats.</p>
  <p>Whether you're new to <strong>Shikaku</strong> or a seasoned solver, GridPaw offers unlimited procedurally generated puzzles across 7 difficulty levels, from Tutorial 3×3 to Legend 8×8. Every puzzle has a unique solution reachable by pure logic — no guessing required.</p>

  <h2>How to <span class="accent">Play</span> Shikaku</h2>
  <p>The rules of <strong>Shikaku</strong> are simple:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The grid contains several <strong>numbered cells</strong>.</li>
    <li>Draw rectangles around each number so the <strong>area equals the number</strong> (a "4" needs a 1×4, 2×2, or 4×1).</li>
    <li>Each rectangle contains <strong>exactly one number</strong>.</li>
    <li>Rectangles cannot overlap, and <strong>every cell must be covered</strong>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>That's it — no math beyond multiplication. The challenge is figuring out which shapes fit where when multiple numbers compete for space.</p>

  <h2>Shikaku <span class="accent">Difficulty Levels</span></h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Tutorial (3×3)</strong> — Learn the basics with guided hints (levels 1–3)</li>
    <li><strong>Easy (5×5)</strong> — Simple puzzles, perfect for beginners (levels 4–10)</li>
    <li><strong>Medium (5×6)</strong> — Introduces more complex rectangle shapes (levels 11–16)</li>
    <li><strong>Medium-Hard (6×6)</strong> — Requires multi-step reasoning (levels 17–22)</li>
    <li><strong>Hard (7×8)</strong> — Advanced deduction needed (levels 23–30)</li>
    <li><strong>Expert (7×7)</strong> — Deep logic chains (levels 31–40)</li>
    <li><strong>Master (8×8)</strong> — For experienced solvers (levels 41–50)</li>
    <li><strong>Legend (8×8)</strong> — The ultimate Shikaku challenge (level 51+)</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Tips &amp; <span class="accent">Strategy</span></h2>
  <p>Rushing to fill cells is the fastest way to get stuck. Instead, work systematically:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Start with primes.</strong> Numbers like 2, 3, 5, 7 can only form 1×N rectangles — their shape is forced.</li>
    <li><strong>Work from corners.</strong> Boundary positions have fewer options, making them easier to resolve.</li>
    <li><strong>Look for the largest number.</strong> Bigger numbers have fewer valid rectangle shapes.</li>
    <li><strong>Eliminate by overlap.</strong> If a placement blocks another number, it's wrong.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Visit our <a href="/tips">Tips &amp; Strategy</a> page for detailed guides on each technique.</p>

  <h2>Why Play <span class="accent">Shikaku</span> at GridPaw?</h2>
  <p>Unlike many puzzle sites, GridPaw has <strong>no ads interrupting gameplay, no paywalls, and no registration</strong>. Every puzzle is free, every solution is unique, and every solve ends with a cute cat celebration. The <a href="/#play">Daily Challenge</a> gives you a reason to come back every day with a fresh 6×6 puzzle and streak tracking.</p>
  <p>If you enjoy Sudoku, KenKen, or other logic puzzles, <strong>Shikaku</strong> offers a refreshing change with spatial reasoning instead of arithmetic. The rectangle partition mechanic is easy to learn but rewards deep thinking — perfect for a coffee break or a long session.</p>

  <h3>Common Shikaku Mistakes to Avoid</h3>
  <p><strong>Forgetting area constraints:</strong> a rectangle around a "4" must cover exactly 4 cells — no more, no fewer. <strong>Overlapping rectangles:</strong> each cell belongs to exactly one rectangle. <strong>Ignoring forced placements:</strong> if a number can only fit one rectangle shape, place it first.</p>
</section>

